How does a major health plan (one of the BUCAs) attempt to assign potential liability to the employer to whom they have a fiduciary duty in general and acting as such under the No Surprises Act (NSA) when they admit to not paying the NSA IDR awards in compliance with federal law?  Read on--full letter posted below:

 1. This BUCA plan states that they are a fiduciary to the employer; fiduciaries have a duty of loyalty, duty of care and a duty of obedience to their principal--the last duty includes the duty of compliance with the law;

2. The NSA requires that independent dispute resolutions (IDR) determinations by a CMS licensed contractor know as an "independent dispute resolution entity" (IDRE), acting under Congressional and CMS authority, must be paid w/in 30 days of the IDRE award; that award process was determined by a neutral third party from physicians and hospitals excising rights under federal law;

3.  Here, the health plan attempts to assign potential liability for their potential breach of fiduciary duty back to the employer who is the principal (last sentence of the first paragraph);

4. In contrast to their duty, the health plan has unilaterally overturned the decisions of the IDREs, admits to not paying and not complying with federal law.  This is from a SEC regulated publicly traded company.  The plan was already determined to be liable and there is simply no legal authority for their unilateral over-turning of the IDRE awards.

5. This health plan has no authority or legitimacy in determining of their own volition that "claims are ineligible"--the IDRE has already determined that they are eligible. 

How SEC regulated entities with express compliance obligations under SEC law, under state DOI laws and as regulated Medicare Advantage contractors believe that they have the license to act in this way is beyond the pale.  (This letter was shared with permission of the party who received it.)
